### `confusable-identifier`

Flag unquoted identifiers made entirely of ASCII characters and a conservative set of Cyrillic lookalikes or fullwidth Latin letters. Report the lookalikes' Unicode code points and the likely ASCII spelling. R treats these spellings as different names.

Greek letters, accented Latin letters, and names containing other non-Latin letters are left alone. Entirely Cyrillic names can still be reported if every letter resembles ASCII. Strings, comments, roxygen, and backtick names are ignored. This is a visual warning, not a ban on non-Latin identifiers or a complete Unicode confusability check; see [Unicode's confusability guidance](https://www.unicode.org/reports/tr39/). No automatic fix is offered because the intended binding is unknown.

This rule is **disabled by default**; enable it with `select`.

Cyrillic `с` looks like Latin `c` but defines a different binding:

```r
с <- 1
```

```text
warning: confusable-identifier
 --> example.R:1:1
  |
1 | с <- 1
  | ^ identifier `с` contains ASCII lookalikes (U+0441); likely ASCII spelling is `c`
  = help: Check which spelling is intended; R treats these as different names.
```

A Cyrillic `а` can hide inside an otherwise ASCII name:

```r
pаyload <- 1
```

```text
warning: confusable-identifier
 --> example.R:1:1
  |
1 | pаyload <- 1
  | ^^^^^^^ identifier `pаyload` contains ASCII lookalikes (U+0430); likely ASCII spelling is `payload`
  = help: Check which spelling is intended; R treats these as different names.
```
